{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1557932531,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWU0NDQ0OTA1MTg=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3416","origin":"github"},"title":"Use cases for `publish --transfer-data`","message":"I think this was originally added out of desperation. The question is whether it has a real purpose?\n\nWhen any paths are given (at least when they are not datasets), I clearly want data transfer.\n\nIf I do not want data transfer, I can only act on the granularity of whole datasets anyways, so I can use `--dataset` instead of a `path`.\n\nI would not be able to specify multiple individual datasets to publish at once in this scenario. However, there is not real requirement to support that, as multiple calls to `publish`, each for a single (root) dataset accomplish the job -- potentially much faster, as these operations are independent of each other.\n\nAll other scenarios I can think of would be covered by adding particular modes for `--force` #3414, including the case that if we want to transfer all data by default, without requiring `.` as path argument, we can have `--force nodatatransfer` as a way to prevent this. Although I am not at all saying we should transfer data by default.","files":null}]}